    description: 'Choose a remodeling contingency based on home age, design completeness, hidden conditions and project complexity—not a universal percentage.', eyebrow: 'Remodel Budget Planning',
    answer: 'Many homeowners begin with a 10%–20% planning reserve, but there is no universal correct percentage. A newer home with complete plans and limited demolition may need less uncertainty than an older, heavily altered house with walls opening and systems moving. Contingency should match risk.',
    intro: 'A contingency is not permission to add upgrades. It is a reserve for conditions and requirements that could not reasonably be confirmed before construction. Treat desired upgrades and incomplete selections separately.',
    sections: [['What contingency is for','Examples include concealed water damage, deficient framing, obsolete wiring, deteriorated piping, inaccessible utilities and necessary corrections to prior work. It can also address quantity or pricing uncertainty that is clearly documented.'],['What it should not hide','A contingency should not replace complete plans, realistic allowances or a defined scope. Known work belongs in the base budget. Optional upgrades belong on a separate wish list.'],['Factors that increase risk','Home age, prior unpermitted work, structural changes, slab cutting, limited access, incomplete selections and unknown utility capacity all support a larger reserve. Investigation before pricing can reduce—but not eliminate—uncertainty.'],['Keep control of the reserve','Track contingency separately and release it only through documented decisions or change orders. Ask whether contractor allowances, contractor contingency and homeowner contingency overlap.'],['Plan before signing','Add construction, selections, professional services and owner costs first. Then apply a risk-based reserve to the categories where uncertainty exists. Avoid borrowing contingency to afford the original scope.'],['ADELIE perspective','Transparent projects identify assumptions early. ADELIE recommends discussing likely hidden conditions, investigation options and change-order procedures before demolition.']],
