    answer: 'Many San Diego remodels need permits when they change structure, electrical, plumbing or mechanical systems. Some limited work may be exempt. Requirements depend on the exact property, scope and jurisdiction, so verify them with the City of San Diego, the County, or the city where the home is located.',
    intro: '“San Diego” can mean the City of San Diego, unincorporated county or another incorporated city. Each authority manages its own applications, fees and local procedures while applying relevant building codes.',
    sections: [['Work that commonly triggers permits','Wall removal, additions, window or door changes, new circuits, panel work, relocated plumbing, water-heater or HVAC work and significant alterations commonly require permits or trade permits. This is a planning list, not a determination for your address.'],['Work that may be exempt','Some cosmetic finishes, cabinets or like-for-like work may be exempt, but related electrical, plumbing, structural or mechanical work can still require permits. The City directs applicants to its municipal-code exemption list before applying.'],['Why permits affect planning','Plan review, corrections, product documents, inspections and closeout take time. Work must remain visible for inspection, so sequencing the trades around required inspections matters.'],['Who should obtain the permit','California home-improvement contracts should identify who will obtain required permits. Be cautious about being asked to act as owner-builder without understanding the legal and practical responsibilities.'],['How to verify requirements','Write a concise scope, identify the jurisdiction, then use the official building department’s project guidance or request a determination. For City of San Diego additions and remodels, Information Bulletin 140 outlines common submittal requirements.'],['ADELIE permit coordination','ADELIE can help connect the design and construction scope to permit planning and identify when design professionals or engineering may be needed. Final requirements are determined by the authority having jurisdiction.']],
